WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.040
So, today we enter the third part of this class, where we talk about robot localization

00:14.040 --> 00:21.120
and mapping. To make sure everyone is on the same page, let's go back to our usual C-Think

00:21.120 --> 00:29.380
Act paradigm to design an autonomous tech. As we said during lecture one, there are many

00:29.380 --> 00:36.320
ways of architecting the autonomous tech. But the most common way it works is to consider

00:36.320 --> 00:43.940
a four-module architecture, whereby we have a module that is devoted to perceive information

00:43.940 --> 00:49.460
and do some pre-processing in terms of filtering, extracting relevant information in terms of

00:49.460 --> 00:54.420
motion primitives, in terms of object detection and so on. Then the information extracted

00:54.420 --> 01:01.360
from the perception module is given to the localization and map building module, whereby

01:01.360 --> 01:06.860
fusing this information and possibly a prior information, the robot tries to understand

01:06.860 --> 01:12.820
what it is in the world and what it is with respect to other objects in the world. Once

01:12.820 --> 01:17.900
we have an understanding of the scene, we know where we are, then we can feed this information

01:17.900 --> 01:22.780
to a decision-making module that is in charge of making high-level decisions. Do I change

01:22.780 --> 01:28.580
the plane? Do I accelerate? Do I decelerate? And so on and so forth. And then this decision

01:28.580 --> 01:34.720
is passed down to a trajectory optimization and control module that is in charge of translating

01:34.720 --> 01:38.420
high-level decisions into actual control for the actuators of the vehicle.

01:38.420 --> 01:43.820
So in module one, we have talked about the trajectory optimization control module. In

01:43.820 --> 01:49.140
the past couple of weeks, we have talked about the perception module. Today and for the next

01:49.140 --> 01:58.460
couple of weeks, we're going to talk about localization and mapping.

01:58.460 --> 02:04.740
Now today, the goal is to learn the foundations of localization and mapping. As we will see,

02:04.740 --> 02:10.860
a natural framework to frame the localization and mapping problem is the framework of probability

02:10.860 --> 02:16.660
theory. Probability theory is one of the prerequisites for this class. But in order to make sure

02:16.660 --> 02:23.660
that everyone is on the same page, I'm going to provide a quick overview of the probability

02:23.660 --> 02:29.900
theoretical results that we need in this class. And then I will use them in order to derive

02:29.900 --> 02:35.260
a foundational result in localization and mapping, which is the result of Bayesian filtering.

02:35.260 --> 02:42.460
Basically, how do we refine our estimates about an uncertain quantity, in our case,

02:42.460 --> 02:47.500
where we are with respect to the world and maybe what the map is, by sequentially ingesting

02:47.500 --> 02:48.500
data?

02:48.500 --> 02:53.260
So we will frame this process through the lens of Bayesian filtering. So I will go in

02:53.260 --> 02:58.300
detail through that, because in the next three or four lectures, everything we will do will

02:58.300 --> 03:03.080
be building on top of this foundational result.

03:03.080 --> 03:10.820
So as I said, localization is one of the most important tasks for a robot. If you don't

03:10.820 --> 03:14.540
know where you are and if you don't know where the objects are with respect to you,

03:14.540 --> 03:18.500
you can be as clever as you want, but you can be neither safe nor efficient.

03:18.500 --> 03:24.500
There are two main approaches to localization, and they are philosophically very different,

03:24.500 --> 03:30.680
in many ways orthogonal. In the behavioral approach, the idea is to design a set of behaviors

03:30.680 --> 03:37.100
that together result in the desired robot motion. So for example, a typical behavior

03:37.100 --> 03:44.300
could be always follow the wall by keeping the wall on the right-hand side. That could

03:44.300 --> 03:51.320
be one behavior. If you do so, chances are that you're going to explore the entire room.

03:51.320 --> 03:55.620
Why the behavioral approach? And of course, you can then have multiple behaviors and mix

03:55.620 --> 03:56.620
them.

03:56.620 --> 04:04.540
So why the behavioral approach is so appealing? Well, one of the key reasons is that it avoids

04:04.540 --> 04:10.280
too explicit reason about localization and mapping. You don't need to build a map in

04:10.280 --> 04:14.620
order to perform such a behavior, and you don't need to localize yourself with respect

04:14.620 --> 04:22.500
to the map. And arguably, it has also some sense of bio inspiration.

04:22.500 --> 04:29.420
So at the end, what this means is that the behavioral approach is fairly easy to implement

04:29.420 --> 04:35.860
and can be very robust. You're making very few assumptions about the world. On the other

04:35.860 --> 04:44.540
hand, typically it does not scale to general environments. While follow the wall by keeping

04:44.540 --> 04:48.940
it on the left-hand side could be a good strategy to visit this room. In general, it's not a

04:48.940 --> 04:54.220
good strategy to visit any location in the world.

04:54.220 --> 04:59.380
So there are a lot of works in the behavioral approach about how to best combine this behavior

04:59.380 --> 05:03.900
in such a way that you retain a computational efficiency, and yet you have some notion of

05:03.900 --> 05:13.920
generalizability. But the approach that we will be focusing on in this class is the so-called

05:13.920 --> 05:21.740
map-based approach, whereby we keep an explicit notion of localization with respect to a map

05:21.740 --> 05:29.820
that can either be known a priori or can be estimated on the fly. Typically, in self-driving

05:29.820 --> 05:34.700
cars, you pre-compute the maps on the environment. Remember the keynote talk we had during week

05:34.700 --> 05:40.540
one of this class for those of you that were in the lecture on Friday. But sometimes, a

05:40.540 --> 05:45.220
robot needs to localize itself with respect to a map that is also estimated at the same

05:45.220 --> 05:49.180
time. This is a problem of simultaneous localization and mapping.

05:50.180 --> 05:55.340
The disadvantage of this approach is that it's more complicated. You need to be able

05:55.340 --> 06:01.220
to reason about a map. You need to be able to reason typically probabilistically about

06:01.220 --> 06:05.960
the localization, about where you are with respect to this map, and so on. So mathematical

06:05.960 --> 06:11.260
is more complicated. Computation is more complicated. Every time you put more assumptions in your

06:11.260 --> 06:16.720
computational framework, you are more fragile and brittle. In particular, one of the key

06:16.760 --> 06:21.960
issues with a map-based approach is that you might have a diverging internal representation

06:21.960 --> 06:25.320
of where you are, meaning that you're seeing that you are in some position, but in reality

06:25.320 --> 06:29.200
you are three meters away, which, of course, for a self-driving car, for example, could

06:29.200 --> 06:36.120
be disastrous. But having said that, it's a very flexible approach. It generalizes well

06:36.120 --> 06:42.280
to basically any environment as long as you can detect landmarks that allow you to localize.

06:42.280 --> 06:48.200
And this is basically the most popular approach for localization. So that's what we will

06:48.200 --> 06:54.480
be doing in this class. But I just wanted for you to know that there are other approaches

06:54.480 --> 06:58.600
that do not rely on the notion of a map localization that are also quite useful.

06:58.600 --> 07:04.640
OK, so in the map-based approach, there are two main equations that need to be addressed.

07:04.640 --> 07:11.320
The first one is, how do we represent a map? Do we represent it at a very granular level

07:11.360 --> 07:18.520
as a sort of a three-dimensional grid, maybe very finely discretized? Or maybe we represent

07:18.520 --> 07:24.280
it in a more abstract way, as a topological map, whereby we abstract away the specific

07:24.280 --> 07:30.400
geometry of the scene, and we just reason about high-level constructs, like, for example,

07:30.400 --> 07:37.360
a table, a wall, and so on, but we don't really discretize the space, the environment.

07:37.840 --> 07:42.320
And each map representation approach has its advantages and disadvantages, and we'll see

07:42.320 --> 07:43.840
them later on.

07:43.840 --> 07:50.200
The second aspect that is what we will be discussing today is the aspect of belief representation.

07:50.200 --> 07:56.640
How do we model the belief regarding the position within the map?

07:56.640 --> 08:01.720
We will discuss next week the issue of map representation. Today, in the following lectures,

08:01.720 --> 08:06.640
we will discuss how to represent the belief, assuming that we have some map representation,

08:06.680 --> 08:12.360
and most importantly, how to update this belief as we get sequential data from our sensors,

08:12.360 --> 08:15.480
such as ladders, cameras, and so on.

08:15.480 --> 08:22.560
The key idea that we were going to exploit in this class, but it is very general in robotics,

08:22.560 --> 08:29.880
is to represent a belief as a probability distribution that, number one, encodes a sense

08:29.880 --> 08:35.880
of a position, and number two, it also maintains an estimate of an uncertainty in that position.

08:36.880 --> 08:41.240
Now, there is a high-level concept.

08:41.240 --> 08:45.160
There are very different ways of implementing this concept.

08:45.160 --> 08:53.080
In particular, depending on what assumptions we make about the belief representation that

08:53.080 --> 08:59.680
we use in our localization filter, we have different types of localization algorithms.

08:59.680 --> 09:05.280
For example, the first choice as a designer is whether I want to consider a unimodeled

09:05.280 --> 09:11.200
belief similar to the grade distribution of this class, where there is a peak, but there

09:11.200 --> 09:12.960
is only one peak.

09:12.960 --> 09:19.200
So you know that most likely you are here, but you have some uncertainty around.

09:19.200 --> 09:27.800
Or you may consider a multimodel distribution, whereby you may have different peaks.

09:28.800 --> 09:33.000
Now, if you're okay with the assumption that you have a unimodeled distribution, that you

09:33.000 --> 09:37.920
brute force in your filter that your belief representation should be, for example, a Gaussian,

09:37.920 --> 09:42.920
and you try to always re-project your estimate into a Gaussian.

09:42.920 --> 09:49.360
Now, obviously a multimodel representation is more flexible, and in reality it is conceivable

09:49.360 --> 09:54.320
that you might have two rough ideas about where you are.

09:54.320 --> 09:59.640
So if I magically drop you in a room at Stanford, you might have an idea that maybe you are

09:59.640 --> 10:02.600
in a video auditorium, or maybe you are in another auditorium.

10:02.600 --> 10:05.960
Those will be the two peaks.

10:05.960 --> 10:08.640
So why people sometimes consider unimodeled distribution?

10:08.640 --> 10:12.800
Well, first of all, as we will see, is computationally more tractable.

10:12.800 --> 10:15.840
And second of all, oftentimes it's a decent approximation.

10:15.840 --> 10:21.840
So for example, if you're following a trajectory, so you know that you're moving locally with

10:21.840 --> 10:26.800
respect to a given trajectory, you know that most likely you're going to be around that

10:26.800 --> 10:27.800
trajectory.

10:27.800 --> 10:32.760
So you don't need to go through the additional hustle of reasoning about multiple peaks.

10:32.760 --> 10:39.640
But airfield engineering is a compromise between how much powerful you want to be in your representations

10:39.640 --> 10:43.160
and how effective that is.

10:43.160 --> 10:48.040
Second choice is whether you want to consider a continuous representation for your beliefs,

10:48.040 --> 10:52.840
meaning that you can be at any point in your environment or a discretized representation

10:52.840 --> 10:57.640
of your belief that would yield an histogram.

10:57.640 --> 11:04.200
Each bar gives a sense of what is the percentage probability that I am at this location.

11:04.200 --> 11:16.360
Say that y is the probability, x is the distance in a one-dimensional space.

11:16.360 --> 11:24.960
Key question in a probabilistic representation about location is, how do we update a belief

11:24.960 --> 11:27.360
based on a set of measurements?

11:27.360 --> 11:33.240
And the key tool we will be exploiting is the tool of Bayesian filtering.

11:33.240 --> 11:38.980
Now the Bayesian filtering is a probabilistic tool, which basically entails that all the

11:38.980 --> 11:43.800
quantities that we're going to deal with in the context of localization and mapping will

11:43.800 --> 11:49.640
be considered random quantities, in particular, random variables.

11:49.640 --> 11:57.240
That is, functions that map the outcomes of an unpredictable process into a real number.

11:57.240 --> 12:02.840
As you know, the name random variable is a bit of a misnomer because the random variable

12:02.840 --> 12:07.000
is technically a function, so it's not a random variable.

12:07.000 --> 12:11.680
But anyway, so you should be familiar with the notion of a random variable.

12:11.680 --> 12:18.480
So as you know, and here is basically the review of the probabilistic tools that we're

12:18.480 --> 12:20.520
going to exploit in this class.

12:20.520 --> 12:24.300
As you know, random variables can be either discrete or continuous.

12:24.300 --> 12:28.800
If they are discrete, random variables are characterized by a probability max function

12:28.800 --> 12:34.520
that assigns the probability that a given random variable capital X.

12:34.520 --> 12:39.440
So here in this class, we refer to a random variable as uppercase, with uppercase letter.

12:39.440 --> 12:44.400
So the probability that capital X is equal to small x is a number.

12:44.400 --> 12:50.160
This is the probability mass function that fully specify the behavior of the random variable.

12:50.160 --> 12:57.300
Typically, one for notational brevity avoids the capital X equal and just writes P of X.

12:57.300 --> 13:02.080
You should always read the P of X as P of capital X equals X, which means probability

13:02.080 --> 13:07.000
that the random variable assumes the values of X is equal to P of X equals X.

13:07.000 --> 13:12.840
This is something that someone gives you or you estimate from data.

13:12.840 --> 13:17.560
Random variables, particularly the probability mass function, is a normalized function.

13:17.560 --> 13:24.020
In particular, the summation over all possible values that the summation of the probability

13:24.020 --> 13:29.660
over all possible values that the random variable can take on is equal to one.

13:29.660 --> 13:34.120
And this is one of the axioms of a probability.

13:34.120 --> 13:41.480
In the case of continuous random variables, the notions are pretty much the same.

13:41.480 --> 13:45.640
Roughly speaking, what to do is to change summation with integrals.

13:45.640 --> 13:49.080
Of course, mathematically it's more complicated than that.

13:49.080 --> 13:54.360
There are a number of nuances that you would discuss in a more advanced class on probability

13:54.360 --> 13:58.760
theory, like the notion of Lebesgue integration and so on.

13:58.760 --> 14:03.760
But for the sake of this class, the main difference is that now with a continuous random variable

14:03.760 --> 14:10.240
doesn't make sense anymore to reason in terms of the probability that capital X is equal

14:10.240 --> 14:11.240
to X.

14:11.240 --> 14:16.960
Since now you have a continuum of possible values of Xs, the probability that a random

14:16.960 --> 14:22.080
variable takes a specific value, small x, is indeed zero.

14:22.080 --> 14:24.360
Remember that probability at zero doesn't mean impossible.

14:24.360 --> 14:29.040
Just mean probability at zero.

14:29.040 --> 14:33.440
So for continuous random variables, the notion that matters is the probability that a random

14:33.440 --> 14:41.040
variable falls within an interval with the lower bound a and upper bound b.

14:41.040 --> 14:45.800
In particular, that probability is written as an integral from a to b of a probability

14:45.800 --> 14:51.800
density function p of x dx, where px to x is the notion of the probability that the

14:51.800 --> 15:01.480
random variable falls within the infinitesimal interval between x and x plus delta x.

15:01.520 --> 15:05.000
So the main difference is that instead of talking about probability mass function, we

15:05.000 --> 15:09.080
talk about density functions.

15:09.080 --> 15:14.920
And as before, probabilities might be normalized, and in particular, the integral over the density

15:14.920 --> 15:29.800
function over the range of values that the random variable can take is equal to 1.

15:29.800 --> 15:31.680
So this is all stuff that you know.

15:31.680 --> 15:36.680
Now we talk about things that you know as well, but they are more interesting.

15:36.680 --> 15:41.360
In particular, in this class, we'll be talking about probability mass functions, so probability

15:41.360 --> 15:44.680
density functions for multiple random variables.

15:44.680 --> 15:45.680
Why?

15:45.680 --> 15:50.680
Because we have to take into account simultaneously about the state, the stream of measurements

15:50.680 --> 15:54.720
we're taking, the stream of controls we're enacting, and so on, and we want to reason

15:54.720 --> 15:56.920
jointly about them.

15:56.920 --> 16:04.600
So we define the joint probability for two random variables x and y.

16:04.600 --> 16:13.520
Probability of, the notation is x and y, by definition as probability of capital X equal

16:13.520 --> 16:20.160
x, and capital Y equal y.

16:20.160 --> 16:24.040
So this is just a definition.

16:24.040 --> 16:29.800
And an important concept, and actually fundamental concept, as we will see later on, is the notion

16:29.800 --> 16:31.960
of independence.

16:31.960 --> 16:40.440
We say that two random variables are independent if their probability mass function or probability

16:40.440 --> 16:47.000
density function factors out.

16:47.000 --> 16:53.480
Now this seems somewhat intuitive, but not obvious.

16:53.480 --> 16:58.400
It will become obvious once we introduce the notion of a conditional probability, that

16:58.400 --> 17:04.120
along with the bias theorem, forms the foundation for Bayesian future.

17:04.120 --> 17:19.760
So let's assume that we know the value that the random variable y is taking on.

17:19.760 --> 17:25.080
Oftentimes a random variable carries information about other random variables.

17:25.080 --> 17:29.160
So it's legitimate to ask the question about how do we assess the probability of x given

17:29.160 --> 17:33.240
that y is equal to some specific value.

17:33.240 --> 17:38.440
This leads to the notion of conditional probability.

17:38.440 --> 17:47.400
Given a value y such that py is greater than zero, the probability of x given y is defined

17:47.400 --> 17:58.280
as the joint probability x and y over the probability of y.

17:58.280 --> 18:02.320
Now also this seems quite intuitive, but it's not obvious.

18:02.320 --> 18:07.040
So first of all, what is important to notice is that this is not a theoretical result.

18:07.040 --> 18:09.360
So this is not something that we derived from other results.

18:09.360 --> 18:11.640
This is indeed definition.

18:11.640 --> 18:18.160
In some context, conditional probability is considered an axiom of probability theory.

18:18.160 --> 18:19.160
So that's a definition.

18:19.160 --> 18:23.760
Let's try to understand a little bit better why this definition makes sense.

18:23.760 --> 18:25.720
This is the first time that I saw it.

18:25.720 --> 18:30.520
It was, well, it kind of makes sense that we are renormalizing by the probability of

18:30.520 --> 18:34.580
events that we know has happened, but it's not entirely obvious.

18:34.580 --> 18:41.520
So consider the example where, so every time you have doubts about probabilistic results,

18:41.520 --> 18:48.040
always reason about the interpretation in the event space, the so-called omega space,

18:48.040 --> 18:50.640
the space of possible outcomes.

18:50.640 --> 18:59.680
So let's assume that we have a rectangle whose area is equal to 1.

18:59.680 --> 19:14.200
So let's define this set as, actually, let's define this set as A.

19:14.200 --> 19:18.680
Let's say that the area is 0.75.

19:18.680 --> 19:36.300
Let's define this set as B and has area 0.5.

19:36.300 --> 19:49.260
And let's define this set as C with area 0.25.

19:49.260 --> 19:58.020
Now if I just throw, without looking something to this rectangle, knowing that I'm always

19:58.020 --> 20:03.180
so good that I always hit the rectangle, it's just random, what is the probability that

20:03.180 --> 20:08.740
I hit the A subset?

20:08.740 --> 20:13.500
75%.

20:13.500 --> 20:17.580
So just throwing randomly means that with uniform distribution, I'm going to hit any

20:17.580 --> 20:19.700
point in the rectangle.

20:19.700 --> 20:28.000
And so in that case, the probability of the event is just the area of that event, 0.75%.

20:28.000 --> 20:32.720
So probability of A is 0.75.

20:32.720 --> 20:51.780
So assume that I tell you that I throw the point somewhere in the area denoted as B.

20:51.780 --> 20:57.760
So the area of B is 0.5, so the probability of B is 0.5.

20:57.760 --> 21:03.340
So now that I tell you that somehow what I've thrown is in B, what is the probability

21:03.340 --> 21:15.020
that I also hit the area A?

21:15.020 --> 21:20.020
So what is the probability that I'm here?

21:20.020 --> 21:22.020
25%.

21:22.020 --> 21:24.020
Why?

21:24.020 --> 21:41.320
So the whole area here is 0.5, this area here is 0.5, this area here is 0.25.

21:41.320 --> 21:42.320
So 50%.

21:42.320 --> 21:43.320
Why?

21:43.320 --> 21:47.400
You know that you are here.

21:47.400 --> 21:51.360
And this area is, well the drawing is not perfect, maybe this one should be more like

21:51.360 --> 21:52.360
here.

21:52.360 --> 21:57.720
The whole area is 0.5, and the overlap is 0.25.

21:57.720 --> 21:58.720
Is how?

21:58.720 --> 22:04.200
Then, since I already know that I'm here, then what really matters is the ratio between

22:04.200 --> 22:09.660
the intersection between the two sets, and the overall set where I'm sure I am.

22:09.660 --> 22:25.860
So the probability of A and B is equal to 0.25, then probability of B is equal to 0.5,

22:25.860 --> 22:38.540
so the probability of A given B is equal to 0.25 over 0.5, it is equal to 1,000.

22:38.540 --> 22:42.540
I mean this is obvious, but sometimes with conditional probabilities one can get confused,

22:42.540 --> 22:45.980
so that's why I want to reiterate the concept.

22:45.980 --> 22:51.980
And again, this is a definition, but the reason why I'm doing this is such an easy example

22:51.980 --> 22:56.260
is to show that this definition is actually very intuitive.

22:56.260 --> 23:01.900
So now that you know that you are confined in a given subset of the sample space, then

23:01.900 --> 23:08.220
it's reasonable to assign to the conditional probability the ratio of what is the size

23:08.220 --> 23:15.700
of that confined sample space with the overlap between the two events.

23:15.700 --> 23:24.660
Of course, if I tell you that I hit C, what is the probability that I also hit a point

23:24.660 --> 23:28.520
belonging to A?

23:28.520 --> 23:31.020
C is this space.

23:31.980 --> 23:32.980
100%.

23:32.980 --> 23:43.620
The overlap is just 0.25 and this area is 0.25, so 0.25 over 0.25 is actually equal

23:43.620 --> 23:45.340
to 1.

23:45.340 --> 23:52.060
So you're just super familiar with this most likely, but oftentimes conditional probabilities

23:52.060 --> 23:57.420
can be a very tricky concept, it's easy to get it wrong, always go back to the sample

23:57.420 --> 23:59.860
space if you have any confusion.

23:59.900 --> 24:03.380
Trust me, it's the only way to clear any confusion.

24:03.380 --> 24:06.820
If you just put together probabilities, sometimes one can get confused.

24:06.820 --> 24:11.540
Again, this is a definition, it's not a theoretical result, but as you see, it's a definition

24:11.540 --> 24:12.540
that makes sense.

24:12.540 --> 24:20.260
Now, in light of this definition, it's now clearer why we define independency the way

24:20.260 --> 24:22.260
we defined it.

24:22.260 --> 24:26.460
Let's assume that two random variables are independent, which by definition means that

24:26.460 --> 24:28.060
they factor out.

24:28.060 --> 24:40.020
If that is the case for independent random variables, probability of X given Y is equal

24:40.020 --> 24:43.060
to probability of XY given over PY.

24:43.060 --> 24:51.420
But since they're independent, those probabilities factor out, so this is PX, PY over PY, so

24:51.420 --> 24:57.500
this is equal to PX, which clarifies why we define independence the way we defined it.

24:57.500 --> 25:03.540
For two independent random variables, the probability of X given Y is simply given equal

25:03.540 --> 25:10.700
to the unconditional probability, which basically means that Y does not carry over any information

25:10.700 --> 25:11.700
about X.

25:16.100 --> 25:20.340
So these are two most important, together with the bias theorem, the two most important

25:20.340 --> 25:21.700
tools we use in this class.

