All right, so an additional tool is the tool of total probability, which is a quite convenient

25:36.660 --> 25:43.220
trick that is fairly obvious, but it will require a proof that I'm not going to give.

25:43.220 --> 25:50.420
Basically, it entails exploiting some of the axioms in probability theorem, in particular

25:50.420 --> 25:58.540
the axiom about combining probabilities of non-overlapping events.

25:58.540 --> 26:11.580
So what this theorem is saying is that probability of X can be written as the sum over all possible

26:11.580 --> 26:21.780
values of Y, of a random variable Y, of P of X, Y, which, by the conditional probability

26:21.780 --> 26:33.540
definition, can also be written as P of X, Y, probability of Y.

26:33.540 --> 26:43.100
So this result for us is mostly a convenient trick, if you will, that says that to compute

26:43.100 --> 26:48.180
the probability of X, assuming we don't know it, but assuming we know the joint probability

26:48.180 --> 26:56.100
of X with another random variable Y, then I can just sum all the probabilities for the

26:56.100 --> 27:01.900
joint random variables X, Y, for all possible values of Y, put them together, and then I

27:01.900 --> 27:04.180
get the probability for X.

27:04.180 --> 27:10.780
The reason why this is useful will become clear in a second when we talk about filtering.

27:10.780 --> 27:16.620
We have obviously the analog for continuous random variables.

27:16.620 --> 27:22.220
As usual, we change summation with integrals and probability mass functions with probability

27:22.220 --> 27:24.700
density functions.

27:24.700 --> 27:30.660
Again, these results will require a proof.

27:30.700 --> 27:33.260
The proof is not so important, so just take it for granted.

27:33.260 --> 27:39.060
But you go on Wikipedia, you have a two lines proof, if you will.

27:39.060 --> 27:41.100
And now we get to the interesting things.

27:41.100 --> 27:48.780
So an interesting fact that follows from the definition of conditional probability and

27:48.780 --> 27:59.300
the axioms of probability is the notion of a bias rule.

27:59.300 --> 28:07.660
The notion of bias rule allows us to invert the dependency, the condition in between a

28:07.660 --> 28:11.060
random variable X and a random variable Y.

28:11.060 --> 28:19.060
Before I state it, so this is basically a consequence of the conditional probability

28:19.060 --> 28:21.700
definition and of the total probability theorem.

28:21.700 --> 28:29.460
Before I state it, it assumes that Y is the data and X is some quantity that we want to

28:29.460 --> 28:30.460
estimate.

28:30.460 --> 28:36.020
Oftentimes, we don't know that distribution.

28:36.020 --> 28:38.380
So what is the probability of a quantity given the data?

28:38.380 --> 28:40.100
That's really what we would like to have.

28:40.100 --> 28:43.860
So for example, what's the probability that we are at position X given the measurement

28:43.860 --> 28:44.860
Y?

28:44.860 --> 28:47.940
That's what we would like to estimate.

28:47.940 --> 28:53.820
And usually, what you have is the probability that tells you what is the probability of

28:53.820 --> 28:58.180
seeing some data given that you are at position X.

28:58.180 --> 29:03.780
So for example, if I am at X, what is the probability that I obtain as a range measurement

29:03.780 --> 29:07.860
for my ladder 30 centimeters?

29:07.860 --> 29:09.740
That's what you have.

29:09.740 --> 29:15.500
So the question is, assuming that you know the probability of your data given the quantity

29:15.500 --> 29:19.140
that you are trying to estimate, for example, your location.

29:19.140 --> 29:23.620
And given that you have some prior about the quantity that you're going to estimate, so

29:23.620 --> 29:28.700
you have some general idea about where you are, how do you estimate the probability that

29:28.700 --> 29:31.460
you are in some location given the data?

29:31.460 --> 29:34.260
That's how we would be using this result.

29:34.260 --> 29:41.660
But as I said, it is a straightforward application of the results of the conditional probability

29:41.660 --> 29:44.060
and total probability theorem.

29:44.060 --> 29:52.260
So in particular, if you want to characterize probability of X given Y, we know that this

29:52.260 --> 29:59.260
is equal to by definition P of X, Y over P, Y.

29:59.260 --> 30:11.260
But P of X, Y is also equal to P of Y given X, P of X.

30:11.260 --> 30:18.780
This is the application of the conditional probability definition, but with respect to

30:18.780 --> 30:23.380
the condition and with respect to X, as opposed to the condition with respect to Y.

30:23.380 --> 30:24.900
So we plug this here.

30:24.900 --> 30:34.980
So what we get is probability of Y given X, probability of X, over probability of Y.

30:35.020 --> 30:41.340
Therefore, probability of Y oftentimes is more convenient to compute it, to use the

30:41.340 --> 30:42.980
total probability theorem.

30:42.980 --> 30:49.620
You don't typically have the probability of seeing the data Y, unconditional or anything.

30:49.620 --> 30:54.700
What you have is the probability of seeing the data given, for example, your location.

30:54.700 --> 31:08.060
So the way you write P Y by using the total probability theorem is to sum over all possible

31:08.060 --> 31:10.540
instances of the random variable X i.

31:10.540 --> 31:16.980
So P of Y by the total probability theorem is equal to summation of X prime of P of Y

31:16.980 --> 31:21.100
over X prime, P of X prime.

31:21.100 --> 31:29.740
The reason why I use X prime here is to differentiate this X, which is the specific value of the

31:29.740 --> 31:35.180
quantity I'm trying to estimate, from X prime that are all the possible values of the quantity

31:35.180 --> 31:36.180
I'm trying to estimate.

31:36.180 --> 31:41.780
So this is just a summation of X.

31:41.780 --> 31:42.900
Any questions here?

31:42.900 --> 31:49.940
So mathematically, it's really application twice of the conditional probability definition

31:49.940 --> 31:54.140
plus the application of total probability theorem.

31:54.140 --> 31:58.620
For continuous random variables, E is the same as usual.

31:58.620 --> 32:01.420
Instead of having probability mass function, these are densities, and instead of having

32:01.420 --> 32:05.860
summation, we have integrals.

32:05.860 --> 32:10.100
This is an interesting result in its own right, but in this class, we are opportunistic and

32:10.100 --> 32:13.940
we only care about theoretical results if they allow us to do stuff.

32:13.940 --> 32:22.220
And what this bias rule allows us to do is Bayesian filtering.

32:22.220 --> 32:26.060
And thus, it has a central role in robotics.

32:26.060 --> 32:34.820
In particular, this rule allows us to infer a quantity through the inverse probability.

32:34.820 --> 32:38.300
We just specify the probability of data, assuming that X was the cause.

32:38.300 --> 32:44.300
Specifically, as I said before, let's interpret Y as the data, as the measurements, range

32:44.300 --> 32:46.420
on the ladders, and so on.

32:46.420 --> 32:51.380
And X as the quantity we want to estimate, for example, location.

32:51.380 --> 32:57.020
Then we can interpret, and this is the same bias rule I had in the previous slide.

32:57.020 --> 33:02.420
We can interpret the bias rule as probability of location given the data is equal to the

33:02.420 --> 33:11.220
probability of the data given the location times a prior over the location over the probability

33:11.220 --> 33:16.020
of the data that we write explicitly through the total probability theorem.

33:16.020 --> 33:17.020
Why?

33:17.020 --> 33:22.540
Again, because typically, you are given the prior, you have a rough idea about where you

33:22.540 --> 33:23.540
are.

33:23.540 --> 33:27.780
Bias filters are iterative algorithms, usually, and so you start with initial condition that

33:27.780 --> 33:31.900
is your rough idea about where you can be, that's the so-called prior.

33:31.900 --> 33:38.020
You are given, for example, by characterizing your sensor, what is the probability of the

33:38.020 --> 33:41.340
data with respect to your location.

33:41.340 --> 33:44.620
That's something that you are tabulating in your computer.

33:44.620 --> 33:51.340
And since you're given these conditional probabilities, you can also compute the probability of the

33:51.340 --> 33:54.100
data by using the total probability theorem.

33:54.100 --> 33:57.700
You know this, you know this, you know this, so you know the denominator, you know the

33:57.700 --> 34:02.580
numerator, hence you can estimate this once you obtain the new data Y.

34:02.580 --> 34:06.540
Fundamental.

34:06.540 --> 34:08.080
Any questions here?

34:08.080 --> 34:11.660
If you understand this, the next five lectures will be fairly straightforward.

34:11.660 --> 34:16.660
If you don't understand this, the next five lectures will be hell.

34:16.660 --> 34:21.020
Because we're going to push this to the extreme.

34:21.020 --> 34:26.660
But the good news is that basically what we're doing is this thing over and over.

34:26.660 --> 34:36.980
Now, notice that no matter what the values of X is, this denominator is always the same.

34:36.980 --> 34:45.780
So typically, a more succinct way of writing the bias rule is by defining this denominator

34:45.780 --> 34:53.540
as one over eta, and just write eta times P of Y given X, P of X.

34:53.540 --> 35:01.220
The reason why we write eta is that this value does not depend on X.

35:01.220 --> 35:05.300
So I might be interested in what is the probability of X equal 3, or what is the probability that

35:05.300 --> 35:07.500
X equal 2.

35:07.500 --> 35:12.460
That is answered by the bias rule, but each time I write the bias rule, I always use the

35:12.460 --> 35:13.460
same normalizer.

35:13.460 --> 35:15.460
So there is no need for you to compute these over and over.

35:15.460 --> 35:21.580
For example, computationally, it would be silly if your code, you recompute this normalizer

35:21.620 --> 35:23.620
over and over.

35:23.620 --> 35:27.860
This may cause some confusion.

35:27.860 --> 35:30.540
So again, there is nothing magic here.

35:30.540 --> 35:35.460
The reason why I just call all this term as a constant eta is that it does not depend

35:35.460 --> 35:37.360
on X, the quantity we're trying to estimate.

35:37.360 --> 35:41.540
Think of this as the variable that we're trying to estimate.

35:41.540 --> 35:42.540
Any questions?

35:42.540 --> 35:43.540
Yeah.

35:43.540 --> 35:49.900
You said you don't need to recompute, but you're going to be getting more data.

35:49.900 --> 35:50.900
So is it not?

35:51.220 --> 35:52.220
Yeah.

35:52.220 --> 35:54.500
Once you have new data, of course you have to recompute it.

35:54.500 --> 35:58.140
I'm saying you don't have to recompute it for all possible values of X.

35:58.140 --> 36:05.180
Your code, what you will be doing is what is the probability of X equal 2, given Y.

36:05.180 --> 36:08.220
Or probability of X equal 3, given Y.

36:08.220 --> 36:11.500
Every time you compute this or this, you should always use the same eta.

36:11.500 --> 36:12.500
So you just compute Y.

36:12.500 --> 36:13.500
Yeah.

36:13.500 --> 36:14.500
Of course.

36:14.500 --> 36:15.500
Yeah.

36:15.500 --> 36:20.740
Because remember, then now we have the dependency with respect to Y, if Y becomes dependent.

36:20.740 --> 36:22.060
OK.

36:22.060 --> 36:28.260
So one more abstract thing, and then we're ready to set up the localization problem.

36:28.260 --> 36:35.340
So we can extend all the results we've seen so far in terms of conditional probabilities

36:35.340 --> 36:45.100
and in terms of independence and so on to the case where we condition on any arbitrary

36:45.100 --> 36:46.100
random variable.

36:46.100 --> 36:47.100
We say Z.

36:47.260 --> 36:54.140
In particular, assume that we know that an additional variable Z takes on the value small

36:54.140 --> 36:55.140
Z.

36:55.140 --> 37:02.300
We define similarly the probability of X given Y Z as probability of Y given, OK, so it's

37:02.300 --> 37:03.300
a bias rule.

37:03.300 --> 37:08.380
Probability of Y given X Z, probability of X given Z, over probability of Y given Z.

37:08.380 --> 37:10.580
So we just keep the conditioning on Z.

37:10.580 --> 37:16.620
And of course I could have more.

37:16.620 --> 37:21.100
And likewise, probability of the independence, we say that the two random variables X and

37:21.100 --> 37:32.500
Y are independent given Z if their conditional probabilities with respect to Z factor out.

37:32.500 --> 37:36.100
So basically these are the same results as before, we just keep these conditionings through

37:36.100 --> 37:37.100
Z.

37:37.100 --> 37:41.340
Again, this is a definition.

37:41.340 --> 37:46.420
What I'm trying to say here is that if we add additional conditioning, then the definition

37:46.420 --> 37:49.860
will stay exactly the same.

37:49.860 --> 37:58.700
Now something that people sometimes confuse is that conditional independence, so the fact

37:58.700 --> 38:08.180
that two random variables X and Y are independent given Z, does not imply absolute dependence.

38:08.180 --> 38:12.740
So you might have two random variables that are conditional independent, but they are

38:12.740 --> 38:15.220
dependent when you remove the condition.

38:15.220 --> 38:20.420
I will see a very explicit example in the robotics context later on.

38:20.420 --> 38:26.780
Likewise, absolute independence does not imply that conditioning on Z, those two random variables

38:26.780 --> 38:33.180
are independent, simply because that Z may carry over some information that relates X

38:33.180 --> 38:37.540
and Y.

38:37.540 --> 38:42.880
You can be the fairly easy counterexamples.

38:42.880 --> 38:48.960
So nothing new here, just saying that if you add conditioning variables, the definitions

38:48.960 --> 38:49.960
stay the same.

38:49.960 --> 38:55.560
So you just have to be consistent in keeping that conditioning.

38:55.560 --> 39:01.600
With addition, don't be confused by the notion of conditional independence and absolute independence.

39:01.600 --> 39:02.600
They're not equivalent.

39:02.600 --> 39:11.200
You may find cases whereby the two do not imply each other.

39:11.200 --> 39:16.320
One more theoretical result, but it's really straightforward, is the notion that the probability

39:16.320 --> 39:20.480
distribution of a random variable fully describes what that random variable is.

39:20.480 --> 39:26.440
But oftentimes, because of computational tractability, we want to have some succinct information about

39:26.440 --> 39:27.440
that probability distribution.

39:27.440 --> 39:29.380
Probability distribution is a function.

39:29.380 --> 39:33.360
So it's difficult to work with, it's a high-dimensional object, and so on.

39:33.360 --> 39:36.200
So you want to find some representative numbers.

39:36.200 --> 39:41.800
Two of the two most important representative numbers are the expectation.

39:41.800 --> 39:47.640
The expectation is defined as the summation of X, the possible values the random variable

39:47.640 --> 39:54.160
can take, times the probability that the random variable takes that value.

39:54.160 --> 39:57.700
It's basically the most likely event, in a way.

39:57.700 --> 40:01.080
For continuous random variables, the same with the integral.

40:01.080 --> 40:07.620
The important thing is that the expectation, since it's defined as the summation over outcomes,

40:07.620 --> 40:09.120
is a linear operator.

40:09.120 --> 40:18.640
Which means that the expectation of A X plus B, where A and B are two given numbers, is

40:18.640 --> 40:25.840
equal to A expectation of X plus B. This is one of the very few results in probability

40:25.840 --> 40:31.820
theory that holds without any assumptions, just by structurally exploiting the properties

40:31.820 --> 40:36.520
of the summation.

40:36.520 --> 40:40.620
If you have a vector of random variables, then the expectation of a vector of random

40:40.620 --> 40:42.660
variables is simply the vector of expectations.

40:42.660 --> 40:46.820
You take the expectation for each component.

40:46.820 --> 40:54.140
So the expectation is one number that you can use in order to more concisely summarize

40:54.140 --> 40:56.920
probability distribution.

40:56.920 --> 41:06.120
The other quantity is the quantity of covariance that encodes the notion of a measure of the

41:06.120 --> 41:09.040
joint variability of two random variables.

41:09.040 --> 41:16.640
And the covariance is defined as, well, as it is written here.

41:16.640 --> 41:20.720
Expectation of X minus expectation of X and Y minus expectation of Y.

41:20.740 --> 41:29.660
Gives an idea of what is the notion of joint variability between these two random variables.

41:29.660 --> 41:35.740
If one moves X in some direction, Y moves in another direction in a completely random

41:35.740 --> 41:38.060
way, then the covariance will be zero.

41:38.060 --> 41:41.580
If instead they are property aligned, maybe because they are aligned on the line, then

41:41.580 --> 41:45.160
you have other one of minus one, if they are normalized.

41:45.160 --> 41:50.740
You can write down that the covariance can also be written in this form, that sometimes

41:50.740 --> 41:53.100
is easier to work with.

41:53.100 --> 41:54.100
Prove this.

41:54.100 --> 41:56.980
It's very simple as an exercise.

41:56.980 --> 42:02.900
Anyway, so key point here is that expectation has the notion of a post-party event.

42:02.900 --> 42:06.660
Covariance is a measure of the joint variability between two random variables.

42:06.660 --> 42:09.700
You should really be familiar with it.

42:09.700 --> 42:14.780
Just making sure that your risk composites are fresh in your mind.

42:14.920 --> 42:17.920
Okay.

42:17.920 --> 42:19.120
Now there is an interesting part.

42:19.120 --> 42:23.360
How do we apply all of this to the problem we care about, which is a problem of localization?

42:23.360 --> 42:27.920
For now, for the next couple of lectures, we assume that the map is given.

42:27.920 --> 42:31.560
So we just want to find ourselves within a given map.

42:31.560 --> 42:35.600
Next week, we'll consider a more complicated problem, whereby the map is not given.

42:35.600 --> 42:40.320
So simultaneously, you have to localize yourself with respect to a map.

42:40.340 --> 42:46.860
Now, at a high level, a robot has two main ways that it can influence the environment.

42:46.860 --> 42:51.060
First of all, the robot influences the environment.

42:51.060 --> 42:56.060
Influence means that, for example, the robot moves in the environment by enacting control

42:56.060 --> 42:57.060
actions.

42:57.060 --> 43:00.380
And at the same time, by influence, I mean interact.

43:00.380 --> 43:04.900
At the same time, it can gather information through measurements.

43:04.900 --> 43:05.900
These are the two main ways.

43:05.900 --> 43:09.380
So either you put actions, and then the robot does something in the environment, or it takes

43:09.440 --> 43:12.280
measurements about the environment.

43:12.280 --> 43:18.320
So in order to describe such an interaction, we accordingly define three key notions.

43:18.320 --> 43:22.480
The notion of a state, the notion of a measurement, and the notion of a control.

43:22.480 --> 43:27.560
In particular, the state is defined as a collection at time t.

43:27.560 --> 43:33.060
So the state at time t, written as x of t, is the collection at time t of all aspects

43:33.060 --> 43:36.960
of the robot and its environment that can impact the future.

43:36.980 --> 43:42.020
So basically, it allows me to predict what will happen in the future, possibly probabilistically.

43:42.020 --> 43:46.580
I'm not saying that prediction has to be deterministic, but probabilistically, I can predict what

43:46.580 --> 43:48.220
happens in the future.

43:48.220 --> 43:52.860
For example, the robot pose, where I am, the orientation, the velocity, location of objects

43:52.860 --> 43:56.620
around the robot, and so on and so forth.

43:56.620 --> 43:58.940
Useful notation is just notation.

43:58.940 --> 44:03.580
The history of the state trajectory between time t1 and t2.

44:03.600 --> 44:10.920
So xt1, xt plus 1, xt plus 1, xt1 plus 2, and so on, is simply written as xt1 colon t2,

44:10.920 --> 44:13.000
the just notation.

44:13.000 --> 44:20.180
We say that the state is complete, and is really oftentimes the definition of a state,

44:20.180 --> 44:25.020
if no variables prior to xt can influence the evolution of future states.

44:25.020 --> 44:27.360
We refer to d as the Markov property.

44:27.360 --> 44:33.560
So what that means is that if I tell you xt, basically where you are, and I tell you what

44:33.560 --> 44:40.900
the robots will do in the future, then you can predict where the robot will be in the

44:40.900 --> 44:41.900
future.

44:41.900 --> 44:46.460
And for that prediction, having additional information, such as previous states, carry

44:46.460 --> 44:48.420
no additional information.

44:48.420 --> 44:50.380
So your prediction will be the same.

44:50.380 --> 44:55.580
Now critically here, I'm not saying that the prediction about the future has to be deterministic.

44:55.580 --> 44:58.940
The prediction in general is stochastic.

44:58.940 --> 45:06.200
For example, due to disturbances in how you move, for example, there are wind gusts and

45:06.200 --> 45:07.200
so on.

45:07.200 --> 45:14.140
But what I'm saying is that information about states prior to t do not affect that prediction.

45:14.140 --> 45:17.400
You cannot improve that prediction.

45:17.400 --> 45:22.560
Measurement, as I said, is a second important concept.

45:22.560 --> 45:29.820
The measurements provide information about a momentary state of the environment.

45:29.820 --> 45:32.660
Measurement could be like a range for a lighter.

45:32.660 --> 45:37.280
So again, the notation is that the trajectory of measurements, so the sequence of measurements

45:37.280 --> 45:41.800
from time t1 to t2, is compatible with t1 colon t2.

45:41.800 --> 45:49.560
And finally, the control actions carry over information about the change of state in the

45:49.560 --> 45:50.560
environment.

45:50.560 --> 45:54.400
So u t1 colon t2 means u t1, u t1 plus 1, and so on.

45:54.400 --> 46:00.600
So something that sometimes people are a little bit puzzled by is the key difference in terms

46:00.600 --> 46:05.280
of uncertainty propagation between a measurement and a control.

46:05.280 --> 46:10.640
A measurement, and the measurement data in particular, tends to increase a robot's knowledge.

46:10.640 --> 46:14.040
Basically, you can constrain, for example, where you are.

46:14.040 --> 46:18.400
A control action tends to induce a loss of knowledge.

46:18.640 --> 46:22.800
The fact that the measurement tends to increase the knowledge of the robot is quite obvious.

46:22.800 --> 46:26.480
It provides constraints on the underlying quantity we're trying to estimate, in particular

46:26.480 --> 46:27.480
where we are.

46:27.480 --> 46:33.080
But why control actions tend to induce a loss of knowledge?

46:33.080 --> 46:42.600
So I know that I could be in this area.

46:42.600 --> 46:43.600
I don't know exactly where.

46:43.600 --> 46:44.600
I could be here.

46:44.600 --> 46:45.600
I could be here.

46:45.600 --> 46:46.600
I could be here.

46:46.600 --> 46:47.600
I could be here.

46:47.600 --> 46:49.240
But I could be anywhere.

46:49.240 --> 46:55.000
So why control action induces a loss of knowledge?

46:55.000 --> 46:56.000
Yeah?

46:56.000 --> 47:02.200
Just saying the control is too moved, then the movement will not be exactly what it is.

47:02.200 --> 47:08.400
So since once we enact a control action, we might have a disturbance, model mismatches,

47:08.400 --> 47:09.960
and a number of stuff.

47:09.960 --> 47:16.520
As we move, say that we are here, actually we might end up here, or we might end up here.

47:17.120 --> 47:22.320
And since I don't know exactly where I am, I have to apply the same process for all possible

47:22.320 --> 47:28.160
points that I could be, which means that basically the set is growing.

47:28.160 --> 47:34.440
So localization, and particular Bayesian filtering for localization, is this kind of ballet between

47:34.440 --> 47:40.280
measurement that decreases uncertainty and control action that increases uncertainty.

47:40.280 --> 47:44.320
All right.

47:44.320 --> 47:49.400
So now that we have the key ingredients, how do we describe their evolution through time?

47:49.400 --> 47:54.160
That evolution through time will become essential in order to establish the filtering equations.

47:54.160 --> 47:57.760
Well, as I said, we consider a probabilistic model.

