So we assume that the state at time t is generated stochastically through a generative model.

48:08.400 --> 48:16.560
In particular, the probability that the state at time t assumes the value x of x t, conditional

48:16.560 --> 48:21.480
on the trajectory of the state, the trajectory on the measurement, and the trajectory of

48:21.480 --> 48:26.680
the inputs is basically, so this probability mass function, probability density function,

48:26.680 --> 48:31.600
is what fully describes the forward time evolution of the state.

48:31.600 --> 48:33.680
Note here the indices.

48:33.680 --> 48:37.280
So this is something that you may get confused.

48:37.280 --> 48:44.720
P of x t depends on the state from 0 to t minus 1, z1 from 1 to t minus 1, and u from

48:44.720 --> 48:46.320
1 to t.

48:46.320 --> 48:47.560
So why is this a typo?

48:47.560 --> 48:50.640
Am I sloppy that I didn't put t minus 1 here?

48:50.640 --> 48:51.640
No.

48:51.640 --> 48:57.640
So the underlying modeling assumption, this is just a convention.

48:57.640 --> 49:01.220
I could also do it in a different way.

49:01.220 --> 49:08.620
The convention is that we get into a state x of t, we get a measurement, and then we

49:08.620 --> 49:11.140
get a control that moves us to the next state.

49:11.140 --> 49:12.780
So that's a sequence of events.

49:12.780 --> 49:16.220
I could do it the other way around, but this is the most natural one.

49:16.220 --> 49:21.340
Get into a state, get a measurement, take an action, and then move to the next state.

49:21.340 --> 49:28.180
The stochastic process that describes that evolution is symbolically represented by this

49:28.220 --> 49:30.940
probability density function, probability mass function p.

49:30.940 --> 49:35.860
Now, a key assumption that we make in this class that is oftentimes but not always a

49:35.860 --> 49:44.820
good assumption is that the state is complete, which means that no information prior to the

49:44.820 --> 49:51.980
current time t minus 1 is useful in order to estimate where I will be stochastically

49:51.980 --> 49:53.980
at the next time step.

49:53.980 --> 49:59.220
What that means in practice is that the probability of x t conditional on the entire history of

49:59.220 --> 50:04.460
the process in terms of states, measurements, and control is equal to probability of x t

50:04.460 --> 50:07.260
given x t minus 1 and u t.

50:07.260 --> 50:13.620
This is an example of exploiting conditional independence.

50:13.620 --> 50:20.260
Conditional on x t minus 1, the probability of x t does not depend on all the other stuff.

50:20.260 --> 50:23.380
Does that make sense?

50:24.300 --> 50:28.220
That's the basic, most important assumption that we make.

50:28.220 --> 50:34.380
In order to predict the state at time t, the only thing that you need to know is where

50:34.380 --> 50:38.580
I am at time t minus 1 and what action I'm taking.

50:38.580 --> 50:41.820
What is that not a good assumption?

50:41.820 --> 50:49.460
First of all, if you augment your state vector in a way that is large enough, you can always

50:49.500 --> 50:54.300
turn any problem into a problem that satisfies the Markov property.

50:54.300 --> 50:57.420
But in general, if you don't do tricks, for example, let's assume that you are trying

50:57.420 --> 51:01.860
to infer the intent of a human.

51:01.860 --> 51:08.340
So when you're trying to change your lane, your action and your prediction about what

51:08.340 --> 51:13.540
the human will do in the future does not depend just on what the human is doing right now,

51:13.540 --> 51:15.580
but what is done maybe for the past two or three seconds.

51:15.580 --> 51:20.380
So if that human has been driving recklessly for the past two or three seconds, then your

51:20.380 --> 51:22.260
prediction changes.

51:22.260 --> 51:28.300
So just the separation distance and the relative velocity in that case is not good enough.

51:28.300 --> 51:33.580
But these are much more advanced and in particular part of my research, the research in my lab,

51:33.580 --> 51:34.580
more advanced application.

51:34.580 --> 51:37.300
For the sake of this class, the Markov property is good enough.

51:37.300 --> 51:42.340
Why does the conditional measurement go away?

51:42.340 --> 51:48.300
Because if I know that the measurements would allow you to pinpoint that you are Xt minus

51:48.300 --> 51:49.300
one.

51:49.300 --> 51:58.780
But if I already tell you that I am Xt minus one, then I'm done.

51:58.780 --> 52:03.660
Similarly to the state equation, so this equation is referred to as the state transition equation,

52:03.660 --> 52:10.580
allows me to transition from the current state, current control to the next state, probabilistically.

52:10.580 --> 52:14.340
This particular model holds for the measurements and in particular we make the same Markovian

52:14.340 --> 52:15.340
assumption.

52:15.340 --> 52:21.660
The probability of the measurement at time t conditional on the entire history of the

52:21.660 --> 52:26.820
robot is equal to the probability of Zt conditional on the current state.

52:26.820 --> 52:34.200
So the fact that the measured range equal to 30 meters, given the time position Xt,

52:34.200 --> 52:39.140
the probability of that measurement is exactly the same whether in the past I came from San

52:39.140 --> 52:41.260
Francisco or I came from San Jose.

52:41.260 --> 52:43.700
This is what it's saying.

52:43.700 --> 52:46.360
This is very reasonable.

52:46.360 --> 52:51.180
So putting everything together pictorially or through the lens of a dynamic Bayesian

52:51.180 --> 52:54.680
network models, that's what happens.

52:54.680 --> 53:00.700
Take a control, we go into the state, we take a measurement, based on the measurement, we

53:00.700 --> 53:05.980
compute a control ut, then we get into Xt and the process repeats.

53:05.980 --> 53:09.540
Note that in general, we don't know the state with certainty.

53:09.540 --> 53:15.520
What we know is, of course, the controls because we decide them and the measurements.

53:15.520 --> 53:19.280
These are latent variables that we do not get to observe, we're trying to estimate them.

53:19.280 --> 53:24.500
So it's also referred to as a hidden Markov model, hidden because a part of the variables

53:24.500 --> 53:32.500
are not accessible to us.

53:32.500 --> 53:40.860
Given this, since we don't have the right access to the state X of t, we're trying to

53:40.860 --> 53:47.020
estimate it, what mathematical objects can we use in order to capture the notion of estimate

53:47.020 --> 53:48.820
about the state?

53:48.820 --> 53:52.460
Well the notion that we use is the notion of belief.

53:52.460 --> 53:57.360
A belief is a distribution that assigns a probability to each hypothesis with regard

53:57.360 --> 53:59.740
to the true state.

53:59.740 --> 54:05.180
So besides the fancy name, the belief is simply a function that tells you, so here's the definition,

54:05.180 --> 54:11.380
the probability of Xt given the history of the measurements and the history of the controls.

54:11.380 --> 54:15.780
So if I give you all the measurements from initial time one to t, and I give you all

54:15.780 --> 54:19.740
the controls, what is the probability that your robot is Xt?

54:19.740 --> 54:25.360
In general, your robot will be anywhere, and for each possible position, I assign a probability.

54:25.360 --> 54:29.840
But complete probability mass function is referred to as a belief.

54:29.840 --> 54:35.320
Turns out that for computational reasons, it is oftentimes useful to distinguish between

54:35.320 --> 54:43.600
the belief that is really the object we want to compute, and then call the notion of estimate

54:43.600 --> 54:45.400
about our location.

54:45.400 --> 54:49.840
We want to distinguish that object from the argument of a prediction belief.

54:49.840 --> 54:54.740
The prediction belief is basically an intermediary object that we will be using, intermediate

54:54.740 --> 54:58.120
object that we'll be using in our filtering algorithms.

54:58.120 --> 55:03.700
The intermediate belief is the probability of Xt as before, conditional on the history

55:03.700 --> 55:09.700
of the measurement and history of the controls, but where the measurements go from one to

55:09.700 --> 55:11.760
t versus one.

55:11.760 --> 55:13.400
So what does that mean?

55:13.400 --> 55:21.560
The belief is our belief about where we are before we account for the most recent measurement.

55:21.560 --> 55:22.980
That's a prediction belief.

55:23.560 --> 55:28.180
In the prediction belief, we account for the most recent control, but we do not account

55:28.180 --> 55:29.980
for the most recent measurements.

55:29.980 --> 55:36.740
In the belief, we account for both the most recent control and the most recent measurement.

55:36.740 --> 55:42.120
The reason why we split these two is that the most general result in order to compute

55:42.120 --> 55:46.780
the belief is a sequential algorithm, where first we account for the control and then

55:46.780 --> 55:47.860
we account for the measurement.

55:47.860 --> 55:50.840
So first we compute the prediction belief, then we compute the belief.

55:50.840 --> 55:52.840
At the end of the day, that's what we care about.

55:52.980 --> 55:59.420
Calculating the belief from the prediction belief

55:59.420 --> 56:01.580
is called the correction or measurement update,

56:01.580 --> 56:02.940
because basically we use a measurement

56:02.940 --> 56:05.380
in order to refine our belief about the state.

56:06.580 --> 56:11.580
Having said that, this leads to the fundamental algorithm

56:11.740 --> 56:15.820
in terms of localization and mapping in robotics

56:15.820 --> 56:17.420
for a map-based approach,

56:17.420 --> 56:19.420
referred to as the bias filter algorithm.

56:20.480 --> 56:21.980
This is the most general algorithm

56:21.980 --> 56:24.060
to calculate beliefs,

56:24.060 --> 56:26.180
and the applicability goes beyond robotics,

56:26.180 --> 56:31.180
can be used basically for any major network model.

56:31.420 --> 56:32.980
The key assumption is that the state is complete.

56:32.980 --> 56:34.660
Basically we have a Markov process.

56:36.460 --> 56:37.700
This is a very general algorithm,

56:37.700 --> 56:40.100
plus basically with no assumptions.

56:40.100 --> 56:44.540
As always, generality means that computation is a concern.

56:44.540 --> 56:47.260
In particular, directly defined,

56:47.260 --> 56:49.620
this algorithm is difficult to implement

56:49.620 --> 56:51.340
and very seldom used.

56:51.340 --> 56:52.700
So I'm going to discuss this algorithm.

56:52.700 --> 56:54.780
What we're going to do in the next two lectures

56:54.780 --> 56:57.500
are basically simplifications to approximation

56:57.500 --> 56:59.300
that make this algorithm computable.

57:00.220 --> 57:02.100
So what does the algorithm work?

57:02.100 --> 57:05.500
So I'm going to go from the very abstract view

57:05.500 --> 57:07.660
to semi-abstract view to the detailed view.

57:08.740 --> 57:10.340
Semi-abstract view.

57:10.340 --> 57:12.940
So this is a two-step iterative algorithm.

57:12.940 --> 57:17.940
We start at time zero with a belief about initial states.

57:17.940 --> 57:19.460
About initial states.

57:19.460 --> 57:22.500
So as all algorithms that are iterative,

57:22.500 --> 57:25.580
we have to start with an initial condition.

57:25.580 --> 57:29.620
So we assume that the belief of X zero is given.

57:29.620 --> 57:30.460
What did it do?

57:32.220 --> 57:34.700
If you really have no clue about where you could be,

57:34.700 --> 57:37.300
you just put a uniform distribution

57:37.300 --> 57:38.700
over the entire Stanford campus,

57:38.700 --> 57:40.860
if you assume you are at Stanford.

57:40.860 --> 57:43.300
Or over the entire surface of the earth,

57:43.300 --> 57:45.860
if you really have an absolute no clue where you are.

57:46.860 --> 57:51.860
Okay, so then the algorithm is iterative.

57:51.860 --> 57:56.580
Given as an input the belief at the time t minus one,

57:56.580 --> 57:58.460
so belief of X t minus one.

58:02.140 --> 58:06.580
So given belief of X t minus one,

58:07.980 --> 58:11.940
and given the most current measurement Z t

58:11.940 --> 58:14.180
and the most current and control U t,

58:14.180 --> 58:19.180
I want to compute the belief of X t.

58:21.860 --> 58:24.260
So I have an initial condition, and I assume,

58:24.260 --> 58:28.180
okay, somehow I know the belief at time t minus one,

58:28.180 --> 58:29.620
and I know the most recent control

58:29.620 --> 58:31.440
and the most recent measurement.

58:31.440 --> 58:33.120
How do we compute belief of X t?

58:34.620 --> 58:38.440
The best we can do within our modeling framework.

58:38.440 --> 58:40.620
So the idea is to use a two-step algorithm.

58:40.620 --> 58:45.620
For each possible X of t,

58:46.220 --> 58:48.260
remember that we don't have direct access to X of t,

58:48.260 --> 58:51.100
so the best we can hope for is for each possible state

58:51.100 --> 58:54.660
where we could be to compute the corresponding probability

58:54.660 --> 58:55.500
we are there.

58:55.500 --> 58:57.300
For each possible X of t,

58:57.300 --> 58:59.420
first I compute the prediction belief.

58:59.420 --> 59:03.100
So basically, I refine my prior

59:03.100 --> 59:05.980
by taking into account the most recent measurements.

59:05.980 --> 59:09.320
And second, I compute a belief by now counting for the,

59:11.060 --> 59:13.380
this is a measurement of control.

59:13.380 --> 59:14.700
Let's repeat it.

59:14.700 --> 59:16.820
So I'm at time t minus one.

59:16.820 --> 59:20.020
The first, so I have the prior

59:20.020 --> 59:22.140
of where I am at time t minus one.

59:22.140 --> 59:24.780
The first step is to refine that prior

59:24.780 --> 59:26.240
to compute the prediction belief

59:26.240 --> 59:29.420
by counting for the most recent control, U t.

59:29.420 --> 59:31.860
The second step is then to account

59:31.860 --> 59:33.660
for the most recent measurements

59:33.660 --> 59:37.380
in order to compute the probability that I am at X t,

59:37.380 --> 59:40.580
given the most recent measurement Z t and control U t.

59:41.420 --> 59:44.220
And then I iterate for all possible values of X t.

59:44.220 --> 59:47.220
So where are these two equations coming from?

59:47.220 --> 59:49.900
So we're going to do derivation,

59:49.900 --> 59:53.020
but the intuition is fairly simple.

59:53.020 --> 59:58.020
So let's say first that we know the belief

59:58.100 --> 01:00:00.180
at time t minus one,

01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:03.520
and we want to understand how the notion of the control U t

01:00:03.520 --> 01:00:06.220
allow us to refine this belief.

01:00:07.220 --> 01:00:10.700
Well, I don't know exactly where I could be,

01:00:10.700 --> 01:00:13.100
but X t minus one,

01:00:15.260 --> 01:00:19.360
so one, or X t minus one,

01:00:19.360 --> 01:00:22.500
two, X t minus one.

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:25.180
And so these are all possible hypotheses

01:00:25.180 --> 01:00:26.560
about where I could be.

01:00:27.580 --> 01:00:29.660
For each one, at time t minus one.

01:00:29.660 --> 01:00:31.540
For each one, I know the relative probability

01:00:31.540 --> 01:00:33.860
that I am there through the notion of a belief.

01:00:34.860 --> 01:00:39.860
Now, assuming these are all possible states

01:00:40.260 --> 01:00:44.060
from which I can arrive at X of t.

01:00:44.060 --> 01:00:45.460
So these are all possible states

01:00:45.460 --> 01:00:49.020
from which, through the control U t, I can arrive at X t.

01:00:50.740 --> 01:00:52.580
So the reasonable thing to do is,

01:00:52.580 --> 01:00:57.120
I know the probability that I could be here.

01:00:58.060 --> 01:01:00.900
And I know, given that I'm here,

01:01:00.900 --> 01:01:03.840
what is the probability that by using control U t,

01:01:04.120 --> 01:01:05.460
I get to X t?

01:01:10.240 --> 01:01:15.240
So overall, what is the probability that I am at X t,

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:18.080
given that I use control U t?

01:01:18.080 --> 01:01:20.520
I consider all possible hypotheses.

01:01:20.520 --> 01:01:22.360
I weight them by the probability

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:25.840
that each one of those hypotheses is true.

01:01:25.840 --> 01:01:27.920
I multiply those probabilities by the probability

01:01:27.920 --> 01:01:30.560
that if indeed this hypothesis is true,

01:01:30.560 --> 01:01:32.200
then I'm going to get to X t.

01:01:32.200 --> 01:01:33.480
Of course, if the probability of going

01:01:33.480 --> 01:01:36.340
from X t minus one to X t, given U t is zero,

01:01:36.340 --> 01:01:39.320
then certainly there is no way I can go to X t

01:01:39.320 --> 01:01:42.080
from these possible values X t minus one.

01:01:42.080 --> 01:01:43.480
And then I combine all of them.

01:01:43.480 --> 01:01:44.480
So I do a summation.

01:01:45.760 --> 01:01:46.600
This is a very high level.

01:01:46.600 --> 01:01:48.480
We're going to derive it more formally.

01:01:48.480 --> 01:01:50.200
But basically, what this equation is saying

01:01:50.200 --> 01:01:54.040
is that the probability that I am at time,

01:01:54.040 --> 01:01:58.120
I am at state X of t is equal to the summation

01:01:58.120 --> 01:02:01.880
of the probability with respect to where I could be

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:03.400
at time t minus one.

01:02:03.400 --> 01:02:05.840
And the probability that, assuming that is true,

01:02:05.840 --> 01:02:08.500
by using control U t, I go to X of t.

01:02:12.960 --> 01:02:14.560
Just to give you some intuition.

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:17.880
What is the probability that I'm here?

01:02:17.880 --> 01:02:20.860
Well, let's look at where I could come here

01:02:20.860 --> 01:02:23.200
from all the possible places

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:25.780
where I could be at the previous steps.

01:02:25.780 --> 01:02:27.520
And let's account for what is the probability

01:02:27.520 --> 01:02:30.320
that from all those previous places, I can come to here.

01:02:31.320 --> 01:02:33.000
And then I sum all of them.

01:02:35.320 --> 01:02:36.760
Okay, so that's the first step.

01:02:36.760 --> 01:02:41.440
The second step is, what about measurements?

01:02:41.440 --> 01:02:44.520
Well, I already know what is the probability

01:02:44.520 --> 01:02:48.700
that I am at X t, given the control U t.

01:02:50.860 --> 01:02:54.200
But then, let's modulate those probabilities

01:02:54.200 --> 01:02:56.600
to see how compatible they are

01:02:56.600 --> 01:02:58.680
with respect to the measurement that I get.

01:02:58.680 --> 01:03:02.000
Maybe I have a very high probability of being X t,

01:03:02.000 --> 01:03:04.200
but then if the probability of getting a measurement

01:03:04.200 --> 01:03:07.080
at Z t given X t is very low,

01:03:07.080 --> 01:03:08.840
then I should re-weight the probability

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:09.920
and bring it down to zero.

01:03:09.920 --> 01:03:13.240
So, say that the probability of being at X t

01:03:13.240 --> 01:03:16.080
before I consider a measurement is 0.7.

01:03:17.120 --> 01:03:19.720
But then, the probability of getting a range measurements

01:03:19.720 --> 01:03:24.240
of 0.3 meters, given that I'm at that value X of t is zero.

01:03:25.460 --> 01:03:27.580
Then, now that I give you this information,

01:03:27.580 --> 01:03:29.780
what is then the probability of being at X t?

01:03:31.020 --> 01:03:32.420
Well, if I tell you that the probability

01:03:32.420 --> 01:03:35.140
that observing that measurement is zero,

01:03:35.140 --> 01:03:37.580
if you are at X t, then you should have played

01:03:37.580 --> 01:03:39.480
the probability with zero.

01:03:39.480 --> 01:03:40.580
Does it make sense?

01:03:40.580 --> 01:03:44.280
So, basically here, we either enhance

01:03:44.280 --> 01:03:47.500
or demodulate probabilities, depending on how compatible

01:03:47.500 --> 01:03:49.500
they are with the measurements we observe.

01:03:50.840 --> 01:03:51.680
Again, this is intuition.

01:03:51.680 --> 01:03:53.540
We're going to do derivation, intuition.

01:03:53.540 --> 01:03:58.540
In reality here, we are using, as we will see,

01:03:59.360 --> 01:04:03.240
the bias rule, and so there is the Nupotlius normalizer,

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:04.360
we'll see in a second.

01:04:04.360 --> 01:04:06.600
Normalizer eta means that this number here

01:04:06.600 --> 01:04:08.800
does not depend on the specific value X of t

01:04:08.800 --> 01:04:09.840
that you're computing.

01:04:09.840 --> 01:04:12.280
Again, here we are iterating with respect to X t,

01:04:12.280 --> 01:04:14.000
so that's why you don't want to recompute eta.

01:04:14.000 --> 01:04:18.000
So, practically what you do is to compute all these factors,

01:04:18.000 --> 01:04:20.080
sum all them, and that will give you the eta,

01:04:20.080 --> 01:04:21.480
and then you just normalize.

01:04:23.600 --> 01:04:26.600
Can you say some, can you say long space?

01:04:26.600 --> 01:04:28.320
Well, you can do it anywhere you want,

01:04:28.320 --> 01:04:29.760
as long as you normalize it,

01:04:29.760 --> 01:04:32.520
and this is a probability distribution,

01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:34.400
so the integral has to be equal to one.

01:04:35.360 --> 01:04:38.180
So, does at least a high-level idea make sense?

01:04:38.180 --> 01:04:39.920
The reason why we're spending so much time here

01:04:39.920 --> 01:04:43.720
is that everything else that we're going to do

01:04:43.720 --> 01:04:48.720
is basically a refinement of this fundamental algorithm

01:04:49.120 --> 01:04:52.100
to make it computable, so something that you can code.

01:04:52.120 --> 01:04:56.920
And in particular, in the problem set in homework four,

01:04:56.920 --> 01:05:01.160
you will be coding a bias filter under the assumption

01:05:01.160 --> 01:05:03.720
that your belief distribution is Gaussian.

01:05:03.720 --> 01:05:06.120
This will be the EKF filter.

01:05:06.120 --> 01:05:09.820
Okay, so let's explain the first step here,

01:05:09.820 --> 01:05:12.720
and then I'll save the second step for Thursday.

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:15.080
So on Thursday, we're going to explain the second step,

01:05:15.080 --> 01:05:16.880
so to derive the second step,

01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:20.960
and then we work on making this filter computable.

01:05:20.980 --> 01:05:22.460
I want to give it more slowly today,

01:05:22.460 --> 01:05:23.380
because I really want to make sure

01:05:23.380 --> 01:05:25.240
that everything here is super clear.

01:05:25.240 --> 01:05:27.540
So before I go into derivation, any questions?

