The first step is the easiest,

01:05:35.300 --> 01:05:39.840
is really a straightforward application of the bias rule.

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:43.060
So the, actually, sorry,

01:05:43.060 --> 01:05:46.380
the second step is the easiest, I will say.

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:49.460
It's a straightforward application of the bias rule.

01:05:49.480 --> 01:05:51.880
The belief of X, T, so by definition,

01:05:51.880 --> 01:05:53.240
the probability of X, T,

01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:55.480
given the history of the measurement

01:05:55.480 --> 01:05:57.400
and the history of the control,

01:05:57.400 --> 01:06:01.060
actually, let me write down the bias theorem.

01:06:08.940 --> 01:06:13.940
Probability of X given Y is equal to P of X, Y over P, Y.

01:06:13.940 --> 01:06:18.940
It is equal to P over Y given X, P X, P Y.

01:06:22.800 --> 01:06:23.720
There's bias rule.

01:06:33.520 --> 01:06:36.440
So this is the definition of the beliefs.

01:06:36.440 --> 01:06:41.440
So now we apply the bias rule with respect to X, T,

01:06:41.580 --> 01:06:45.480
with respect to X, T, and Z, T.

01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:49.420
So in particular, probability of X, so we keep,

01:06:50.660 --> 01:06:54.060
so the variable, the extra conditioning variables

01:06:54.060 --> 01:06:57.380
will be Z1 to T minus one, and U1 to T.

01:06:57.380 --> 01:07:00.460
And we apply bias rule with respect to X, T and Z, T.

01:07:01.420 --> 01:07:02.780
Okay, so apply bias rule.

01:07:04.580 --> 01:07:09.580
Apply bias to X, T, and Z, T.

01:07:12.060 --> 01:07:15.980
So X, T will be basically here,

01:07:15.980 --> 01:07:18.260
and Z, T will be the Y here.

01:07:18.260 --> 01:07:20.980
All the other variables are extra conditioning variables

01:07:20.980 --> 01:07:23.640
that we keep, as we discussed before.

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:26.860
So probability of X, T given Z, T, Z1,

01:07:26.860 --> 01:07:29.900
all the way to T minus one, U1 all the way to T,

01:07:29.900 --> 01:07:33.100
is equal to probability of Z, T given X, T,

01:07:33.100 --> 01:07:34.520
and all the extra stuff,

01:07:34.520 --> 01:07:37.620
probability of X, T over probability of Z, T.

01:07:37.620 --> 01:07:40.160
And we keep the extra conditioning variables.

01:07:41.920 --> 01:07:46.060
Note that this quantity does not depend

01:07:46.060 --> 01:07:48.880
on the specific value of X, T.

01:07:48.880 --> 01:07:51.380
So this is our normalizer that we just defined

01:07:51.380 --> 01:07:52.660
as eta minus one.

01:07:55.340 --> 01:07:57.220
So what is this object?

01:07:57.220 --> 01:07:59.520
So this is the probability of Z, T,

01:07:59.520 --> 01:08:04.520
so of the current measurement, given the state,

01:08:05.100 --> 01:08:07.560
the previous measurement, and the controls.

01:08:07.560 --> 01:08:12.180
Remember that by the Markov assumption,

01:08:13.440 --> 01:08:16.380
the probability of seeing a measurement Z, T

01:08:16.380 --> 01:08:19.520
only depends on the current state and nothing else.

01:08:19.520 --> 01:08:21.940
In particular, previous measurements,

01:08:21.940 --> 01:08:23.420
given the current state,

01:08:23.420 --> 01:08:26.360
given that we know the current state,

01:08:26.360 --> 01:08:27.780
do not tell us anything more

01:08:27.780 --> 01:08:29.980
in terms of how to predict the measurement.

01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:35.580
So here, to go from here to here,

01:08:35.580 --> 01:08:37.020
I apply the Markov property.

01:08:37.560 --> 01:08:41.320
Again, this is critical.

01:08:41.320 --> 01:08:44.020
So what it's saying is that the conditional,

01:08:44.020 --> 01:08:46.100
and this is the notion of conditional independence,

01:08:46.100 --> 01:08:49.580
conditional X, T, all the extra information

01:08:49.580 --> 01:08:52.460
about the previous measurements and controls,

01:08:52.460 --> 01:08:55.540
do not carry any additional information about Z, T.

01:08:55.540 --> 01:08:58.200
This is the assumption that we're making.

01:08:58.200 --> 01:09:00.980
So if we believe in this assumption, this is correct,

01:09:00.980 --> 01:09:02.900
otherwise it's not correct.

01:09:02.900 --> 01:09:05.340
Usually this is a good assumption, so this works.

01:09:06.000 --> 01:09:08.200
What about the second term?

01:09:08.200 --> 01:09:09.840
So this is the probability of X, T,

01:09:09.840 --> 01:09:12.720
given all the measurement up to T minus one,

01:09:12.720 --> 01:09:14.340
so up to the previous step,

01:09:14.340 --> 01:09:16.680
and all the controls up to time T,

01:09:16.680 --> 01:09:20.020
so up to the previous step and the current control.

01:09:20.020 --> 01:09:24.220
By definition, this is the prediction belief,

01:09:24.220 --> 01:09:25.500
the belief about the states

01:09:25.500 --> 01:09:28.120
will account for the most recent control,

01:09:28.120 --> 01:09:30.440
but not the most recent measurement.

01:09:30.440 --> 01:09:32.000
So what we have just found out

01:09:32.000 --> 01:09:34.640
is that the relation between the belief

01:09:34.780 --> 01:09:38.460
and the prediction belief is through a multiplicative factor

01:09:38.460 --> 01:09:40.860
that accounts for the most recent measurement.

01:09:43.180 --> 01:09:45.880
This is something that we assume we know,

01:09:45.880 --> 01:09:47.900
so we have characterized our sensor in a way,

01:09:47.900 --> 01:09:49.780
so if I'm in a certain location,

01:09:49.780 --> 01:09:52.740
I can predict what type of measurements I could get.

01:09:54.620 --> 01:09:58.060
This is something that gets updated recursively.

01:09:58.060 --> 01:10:02.540
So this comes from, so this will be step two

01:10:02.560 --> 01:10:03.920
of the algorithm.

01:10:03.920 --> 01:10:06.280
The prediction belief is computed in step one.

01:10:09.400 --> 01:10:10.960
And eta is the normalizer.

01:10:10.960 --> 01:10:11.960
Any questions here?

01:10:17.760 --> 01:10:20.680
Well, okay, so it's time, let's do the first step.

01:10:20.680 --> 01:10:25.680
So this seems slightly more involved, but not really.

01:10:28.880 --> 01:10:30.960
So again, let's start from the definition

01:10:30.980 --> 01:10:33.240
of the quantity that we're trying to characterize

01:10:33.240 --> 01:10:34.580
with respect to what we already know.

01:10:34.580 --> 01:10:36.040
Again, what do we know?

01:10:36.040 --> 01:10:40.020
Is we assume that we know the belief at time t minus one,

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:42.380
and we know the state transition model,

01:10:43.740 --> 01:10:45.780
and we know the measurement model.

01:10:45.780 --> 01:10:47.060
In the first step, the measurement model

01:10:47.060 --> 01:10:48.620
actually doesn't play any role.

01:10:49.780 --> 01:10:53.100
So the prediction belief is the probability

01:10:53.100 --> 01:10:55.380
of the state being equal to xt

01:10:55.380 --> 01:10:56.980
given the history of the measurements

01:10:56.980 --> 01:10:59.120
and the history of the controls.

01:10:59.140 --> 01:11:01.820
But a total probability law,

01:11:01.820 --> 01:11:03.260
and again, we use it as a trick

01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:06.340
to make computation more tractable,

01:11:06.340 --> 01:11:10.580
we express this probability by adding the conditioning

01:11:10.580 --> 01:11:12.780
with respect to xt minus one.

01:11:12.780 --> 01:11:15.240
What we do so from intuition standpoint

01:11:15.240 --> 01:11:18.220
is because we want to leverage the knowledge we have

01:11:18.220 --> 01:11:20.340
about the transition function.

01:11:20.340 --> 01:11:22.140
So the knowledge that we assume we have

01:11:22.140 --> 01:11:24.500
about how to predict the state at time t

01:11:24.500 --> 01:11:26.640
given that we are at state t minus one

01:11:26.640 --> 01:11:27.980
and we not control ut.

01:11:28.880 --> 01:11:30.400
We're trying to relate what we want to compute

01:11:30.400 --> 01:11:31.300
with what we know.

01:11:32.460 --> 01:11:35.080
So we apply the total probability theorem

01:11:35.080 --> 01:11:38.440
by using as, by considering extra conditioning variable

01:11:38.440 --> 01:11:39.400
xt minus one.

01:11:39.400 --> 01:11:44.120
Remember, total, I like to write it in a simple form

01:11:44.120 --> 01:11:46.980
to show you that even though there is a quadrant there,

01:11:46.980 --> 01:11:48.300
the concept is easy.

01:11:48.300 --> 01:11:50.120
Remember that probability of x

01:11:50.120 --> 01:11:52.520
is equal to the summation over y

01:11:52.520 --> 01:11:56.980
over p of xy equal to summation over y

01:11:57.000 --> 01:12:02.000
over p of, sorry, x given y py.

01:12:07.480 --> 01:12:09.240
This is exactly what I'm doing here.

01:12:09.240 --> 01:12:10.640
The clatter is that we have additional

01:12:10.640 --> 01:12:12.480
conditioning variables.

01:12:12.480 --> 01:12:13.920
But I'm adding the extra condition

01:12:13.920 --> 01:12:15.120
with respect to xt minus one.

01:12:15.120 --> 01:12:17.280
So probability of xt is the summation but integral

01:12:17.280 --> 01:12:19.880
because we're assuming here we are in continuous spaces.

01:12:19.880 --> 01:12:21.660
So probability of xt given xt minus one

01:12:21.660 --> 01:12:23.380
and the history as usual,

01:12:23.380 --> 01:12:26.280
probability of xt minus one and the history as usual.

01:12:26.300 --> 01:12:27.700
Probability.

01:12:27.700 --> 01:12:28.920
Now we apply Markov.

01:12:28.920 --> 01:12:31.460
So Markov says that the probability

01:12:31.460 --> 01:12:34.220
of being in state x of t given xt minus one

01:12:34.220 --> 01:12:36.820
and the most recent control u t.

01:12:36.820 --> 01:12:41.060
So that probability does not get any,

01:12:41.060 --> 01:12:42.700
systematically the quantity does not get

01:12:42.700 --> 01:12:45.940
any additional information given xt minus one and u t

01:12:45.940 --> 01:12:48.900
if we assume that we also know all the previous measurements.

01:12:48.900 --> 01:12:51.140
Knowing the previous state t minus one

01:12:51.140 --> 01:12:53.620
and the current control is all we need

01:12:53.620 --> 01:12:55.980
in order to estimate x of t.

01:12:56.520 --> 01:12:57.460
So that's a Markov property again.

01:12:57.460 --> 01:12:59.000
Before we applied it for the measurements,

01:12:59.000 --> 01:13:01.820
now we are applying it for the state propagation.

01:13:03.280 --> 01:13:04.380
Fundamental concept.

01:13:04.380 --> 01:13:08.400
So from here, so this object, to go to this object,

01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:09.800
apply the Markov property.

01:13:11.700 --> 01:13:16.700
Now, this project, this component did not change.

01:13:18.120 --> 01:13:20.700
So I just applied a Markov property here.

01:13:20.700 --> 01:13:24.520
The last one, and not tricky,

01:13:24.520 --> 01:13:28.980
but a little bit subtle, is that now we have,

01:13:28.980 --> 01:13:33.500
so this first component, we keep it equal.

01:13:33.500 --> 01:13:36.100
So this is a quantity where we assume we know.

01:13:36.100 --> 01:13:39.140
So this is basically the state transition model.

01:13:39.140 --> 01:13:41.100
And we already talked about it, for example,

01:13:41.100 --> 01:13:42.540
during lecture one.

01:13:42.540 --> 01:13:45.740
This really related to the kinematic model of your vehicle.

01:13:45.740 --> 01:13:47.540
If another state, another control,

01:13:47.540 --> 01:13:50.100
you know what will be in the future.

01:13:50.100 --> 01:13:52.220
Difference here is that we are reasoning probabilistically

01:13:52.720 --> 01:13:54.640
as opposed to the deterministic model

01:13:54.640 --> 01:13:57.620
we were considering in lecture one.

01:13:57.620 --> 01:13:59.040
So the subtle thing is here.

01:13:59.040 --> 01:14:01.640
So here I have probability of x t minus one,

01:14:01.640 --> 01:14:02.920
given the history of the measurement

01:14:02.920 --> 01:14:06.600
up to time t minus one, and all the controls.

01:14:06.600 --> 01:14:09.040
I'm saying that this probability

01:14:09.040 --> 01:14:10.480
is equal to this probability,

01:14:10.480 --> 01:14:12.560
whereby I still have x t minus one.

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:14.580
I still have all the measurements.

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:18.560
And I condition all the controls up to t minus one, not t.

01:14:18.560 --> 01:14:21.360
So basically I removed the condition with respect to t.

01:14:22.300 --> 01:14:24.180
So why is that so?

01:14:28.800 --> 01:14:32.600
Well, so the thing is, if you think about the

01:14:34.440 --> 01:14:37.300
dynamic bias net interpretation.

01:14:39.740 --> 01:14:43.260
So we have u t minus one.

01:14:44.360 --> 01:14:46.660
We go into x t minus one.

01:14:46.800 --> 01:14:51.800
We get z t minus one, and then we get a new control, u t,

01:14:56.080 --> 01:14:57.440
then go into x t.

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:04.480
But we're trying to estimate x t minus one.

01:15:05.980 --> 01:15:09.160
So the fact that I tell you the control that I will apply

01:15:09.160 --> 01:15:11.960
in order to transition to x t,

01:15:11.960 --> 01:15:16.120
what type of possible information can carry

01:15:16.140 --> 01:15:18.420
in order to better estimate x t minus one.

01:15:18.420 --> 01:15:22.040
At best, u t is computed by using information

01:15:22.040 --> 01:15:24.900
from z t minus one and previous measurements.

01:15:24.900 --> 01:15:27.340
So it's basically a function of stuff

01:15:27.340 --> 01:15:29.980
that I'm already conditioning over.

01:15:31.540 --> 01:15:33.200
So u t is usually controlled,

01:15:34.400 --> 01:15:36.220
so the control is a function of the measurements

01:15:36.220 --> 01:15:37.900
that I've received so far.

01:15:37.900 --> 01:15:42.220
So typically u t depends on all the previous measurements,

01:15:42.220 --> 01:15:45.280
or most practically the previous measurements only.

01:15:45.300 --> 01:15:47.900
So if I already tell you z t minus one,

01:15:47.900 --> 01:15:51.580
knowing u t does not carry over any additional information.

01:15:51.580 --> 01:15:54.500
So that's why these two probabilities

01:15:54.500 --> 01:15:56.300
are actually a dense plot.

01:15:56.300 --> 01:15:57.600
Does it make sense?

01:15:57.600 --> 01:16:00.540
So here what I'm saying is that u t does not carry over

01:16:00.540 --> 01:16:03.640
any additional information to estimate x t minus one.

01:16:03.640 --> 01:16:05.640
The reason being is that u t,

01:16:06.540 --> 01:16:08.340
for general output feedback policies,

01:16:08.340 --> 01:16:10.960
that is control policies that are a function

01:16:10.960 --> 01:16:12.420
of the measurements,

01:16:12.440 --> 01:16:14.280
so that u t will be a function of z t minus one

01:16:14.280 --> 01:16:15.320
and previous one.

01:16:15.320 --> 01:16:20.320
So it will just be a more compressed version

01:16:20.600 --> 01:16:21.720
of all the previous measurements,

01:16:21.720 --> 01:16:24.020
but doesn't carry over any additional information.

01:16:25.240 --> 01:16:28.160
Okay, but if that is so,

01:16:28.160 --> 01:16:30.480
what is by definition probability of x t minus one,

01:16:30.480 --> 01:16:32.520
given measurement up to t minus one

01:16:32.520 --> 01:16:34.640
and control up to t minus one?

01:16:34.640 --> 01:16:37.400
That's by definition is the belief at time t minus one.

01:16:37.400 --> 01:16:42.400
And now let's go from the start to the end.

01:16:44.740 --> 01:16:46.300
This is really step number one.

01:16:47.720 --> 01:16:49.580
So I gave you a derivation for these two steps.

01:16:49.580 --> 01:16:52.220
I also gave you the intuition.

01:16:52.220 --> 01:16:55.060
So what we'll do next time will be to

01:16:59.360 --> 01:17:01.060
start from the bias filter,

01:17:01.060 --> 01:17:02.180
but now make it computable.

01:17:02.180 --> 01:17:03.820
So making approximations is such a way

01:17:03.820 --> 01:17:06.100
that we can use it in real time

01:17:06.100 --> 01:17:09.080
in order to estimate the quantity we care about

01:17:09.080 --> 01:17:09.920
in this context,

01:17:09.920 --> 01:17:13.040
which is localizing the robot with respect to the map.

01:17:13.040 --> 01:17:14.460
So I'll see you on Thursday.

